## Quickstart
<hr class="section-rule lvl-2">

### Basic Installation
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Just copy the `compose.mk` file and  make it executable:

```bash {.cli_example}

$ cd myproject 

# Download the compose.mk automation lib
$ curl -sL \
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Robot-Wranglers/compose.mk/main/compose.mk \
    > compose.mk

# Make it executable
$ chmod +x compose.mk

# Now equivalent to `make -f compose.mk ...`
$ ./compose.mk ...
```

If you have `make` and `docker`, that's all the setup you'll need for stand-alone [tool mode]({{_}}/tool-overview) usage.  

Note that since [various containers]({{_}}/tool-overview/#tool-wrappers) are only pulled on demand, initial usage might be slow.  *(For example, you might need a `jq` container before `compose.mk` can work with JSON)*.  Later runs will take advantage of cache.

If you want to keep `compose.mk` out of your project root, you could use a `.cmk/` folder, and while it's not really necessary or recommended.. you could also track upstream with a git submodule. *(See the [Plugins, Forks, and Versioning section](#forks-versioning) for more details.)*

### Global Install
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Besides the vendored drop-in above, `compose.mk` ships package-manager shims under {{macros.repo_link('the `via/` folder', name='via/', github=github)}} which install to `PATH`.  Currently supported, mostly untested, contributions welcome:

* {{macros.repo_link('via/basher', name='basher', github=github)}}
* {{macros.repo_link('via/npm', name='npm', github=github)}}
* {{macros.repo_link('via/pip', name='pip', github=github)}}
* {{macros.repo_link('via/docker', name='docker', github=github)}} (runs `compose.mk` *from a container*, see [below](#run-from-a-container))

Pip has good support for the common use-cases, but see also [these remarks](#forks-versioning) about versioning.  You can install from the repo subdirectory (no PyPI package needed):

```bash {.cli_example}
# Install from the `via/pip` subdir on the default branch:
$ pip install "git+https://github.com/Robot-Wranglers/compose.mk.git#subdirectory=via/pip"

# Pin to a specific branch (or tag / commit) with `@<ref>`:
$ pip install "git+https://github.com/Robot-Wranglers/compose.mk.git@main#subdirectory=via/pip"
```

After a global install you can `include` compose.mk by its resolved path (as found on your `PATH`) from any Makefile, anywhere.

Read on for details about tighter integration with projects, and see the [compatibility notes](#compatibility-notes) for details about docker versions / possible gotchas in OSX.

### Run from a Container
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Rather than installing anything on the host, you can run `compose.mk` *from a container* using the {{macros.repo_link('via/docker', name='via/docker', github=github)}} shim.  Build the image straight from a git ref + subdirectory (no checkout needed):

```bash {.cli_example}
$ docker build -t compose.mk \
    https://github.com/Robot-Wranglers/compose.mk.git#main:via/docker
```

Because `compose.mk`'s whole model is running tools *in containers*, a containerized `compose.mk` needs the **host docker socket shared** so it can start those as *sibling* containers on the host daemon:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ docker run --rm -it \
    -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \
    -v "$PWD:$PWD" -w "$PWD" \
    -e DOCKER_HOST_WORKSPACE="$PWD" \
    compose.mk flux.ok
```

!!! warning "Mount at the same path"
    Mount your project at its *own* host path (`-v "$PWD:$PWD" -w "$PWD"`), not remapped to `/workspace`.  Sibling tool-containers are created by the *host* daemon, so any path compose.mk hands to docker must be valid on the host; same-path mounting makes in-container paths equal host paths.  (Remapping to `/workspace` is fine for trivial host-only targets but breaks container-dispatch.)  See the README for running *your* project's dispatch targets.

The image is also `FROM`-able as a base for your own app, and its `--target test` stage can run compose.mk's own test suite from inside the container.  See the {{macros.repo_link('via/docker README', name='via/docker README', github=github)}} for those plus prebuilt GHCR images and caveats (file ownership, `DOCKER_SOCKET`).  See also the [raw-docker docs]({{_}}/raw-docker) for how dispatch and the shared socket work.

### Project Integration
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

For using the [standard library]({{_}}/standard-lib), just write `include compose.mk` in your Makefile.  If you brought your own tool containers, you'll probably be interested in setting up the [Make/Compose Bridge]({{_}}/bridge), preparing for [Container Dispatch]({{_}}/container-dispatch), or using [docker more directly]({{_}}/raw-docker).  

See that full documentation for more details, or if you already know what you're doing, read on for the copy/paste stuff.

#### Makefile Integration
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

A quick example of what your project Makefile could look like:

```Makefile {.snippet .language-Makefile}
# Include compose.mk for access to target API and macros.
include compose.mk

# If you brought tool containers, you'll want to import them.
# Call the bridge-building macro to get targets for each service.
$(call compose.import, file=docker-compose.yml)

# At this point, scaffolded targets are defined for 
# the tool containers mentioned in the compose file.
# Now you can dispatch any task to any tool container.
test: tool_container_name.dispatch/self.test

self.test:
	echo hello world from container `uname -n -v`
```

#### Docker Compose Integration
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Working with docker compose is optional if you're just interested in [stand-alone mode]({{_}}/tool-overview/) or the [standard library offerings]({{_}}/standard-lib).  And if you *are* using docker-compose, then simple access to tools-containers from your compose files will *just work* for many use-cases, via the [Makefile integration](makefile-integration) in the last section and via the [scaffolded targets]({{_}}/bridge).  But, more complicated use-cases will usually need a volume-mount for your project directory so that tool containers can read/write files, and, [using container dispatch]({{_}}/container-dispatch) means that your tool containers need `make` and `bash`.

Here is some fairly complete boilerplate that's usually a good starting place:

{{site.embed_demo('demos/data/docker-compose.yml', github=github, class='{.snippet}', type='yaml')}}

This example above is typical, but not necessarily minimal, so let's discuss a few things about it.  

**Regarding base images,** there's a good chance your tool-container already includes `make`, and you don't need to add packages at all.  But if you're looking for reasonable base-containers that include `make` and will help you avoid initial package updates with `apt` or `apk`.. you might consider things like [golang containers](https://hub.docker.com/_/golang/tags), [gcc containers](https://hub.docker.com/_/gcc/), or [`debian/buildd:bookworm`](https://packages.debian.org/bookworm/buildd).

!!! tip "Tips for configuring docker-compose"
    1. **Use defaults-with-overrides in `FROM` lines** to stay versioned but still keep some flexibility. 
    1. **Use `dockerfile_inline` to quickly extend a base-container**, without adding an extra Dockerfile to your repository.  Tool-containers rarely change once established, and having a separate file and separate git/docker repos for everything can get tedious.  
    1. **Use yaml inheritance with anchors,** i.e. `&base` and `<<: *base`.  This is a good trick to keep compose files DRY and as simple as possible.  
    1. **Provide a few optional keys just for clarity.** Things like `hostname` clean up logging, and even if `entrypoint` isn't used by software it can help humans who are trying to debug.
    1. **Correct config keys for `working_dir` and `volumes` are crucial for project file IO,** which might include reading your project `Makefile` if you're using container dispatch.
    1. It's optional, but **there are cases where you'll want `docker` to be available inside tool-containers** and you also might want to share the hosts docker-socket.[^1]  The boilerplate above goes half way, defaulting to using a volume mount but stopping short of actually installing docker.  

### Compatibility Notes
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Platforms used in development for `compose.mk` include recentish docker, recentish make (*3.8+*) and bash (*5+*) on both Linux and MacOS.  Testing done in CI only uses Linux and won't try every possible combination of versions, but the goal is to support most things you'll encounter in the wild.

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

**For docker,** recentish means a client at least 25+, engine at 20+ and while it's not a strict requirement, using docker-compose at 2.17.0+ is *strongly recommended*.  Otherwise you won't have access to `dockerfile_inline`, which is inconvenient and also breaks many demos.

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

**For MacOS,** the [standard library]({{_}}/standard-lib) should mostly Just Work.  For more [advanced]({{_}}/cmk/compiler) [features]({{_}}/bridge) though, there are the usual problems with BSD vs GNU tools, particularly `sed` and `awk`.

Typically `/usr/local/bin` comes before `/usr/bin` in PATH, so this should leave your base installation untouched and still prefer GNU by default:

```bash {.cli_example}
# Install a GNU awk
$ brew install gawk
$ cp /usr/local/bin/gawk /usr/local/bin/awk

# Install a GNU sed
$ brew install gnu-sed
$ cp /usr/local/bin/gsed /usr/local/bin/sed
```

Usage of other tools like `xargs` should be BSD/GNU agnostic so please report any issues!  Opting in to GNU tools on MacOS *everywhere* is a matter of taste, but might be worthwhile. See documentation for example here[^3].

Another edge case is that docker desktop in OSX is *increasingly problematic in general*, and also officially unsupported these days if you're still on Intel instead of Apple silicon.  Rancher-desktop is one possible work around.

### For Developers
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

For development on `compose.mk` itself, here's how you can clone the project, build related containers, and run the test suites.

```bash {.cli_example}
# Clone with SSH
$ git clone --recursive git@github.com:Robot-Wranglers/compose.mk.git

# Or clone with HTTP
$ git clone --recursive https://github.com/Robot-Wranglers/compose.mk.git

# Pull tool containers to do some early caching
$ make clean build 

# Run all demos (in Makefile)
$ make demos
```

To exercise the [cmk-lang]({{_}}/cmk/compiler) features (compilers, interpreters, and special syntax), run the integration suite, which drives the CMK demos through the real interpreter with assertions:

```bash {.cli_example}
# Run the integration suite (covers the cmk-lang demos)
$ make integration-test
```

Any individual demo can also be run directly via its shebang, e.g. `./demos/cmk/underload.cmk`.

### Plugins, Forks, & Versioning
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">
<a name=forks-version></a><a name=forks-versioning></a>

Given the advice above to simply embed `compose.mk` directly into your project repositories, some people might be reading this and thinking **what if compose.mk changes upstream?**  The [global-install shims](#global-install) can help here too, but for a vendored copy, the main things you can do are:

**Fork-and-Forget:**
:   The [standard library]({{_}}/standard-lib) for `compose.mk` is approaching *frozen*, so especially if you're not using [cmk-lang]({{_}}/cmk/compiler), this is a pretty reasonable option.
: &nbsp;

**Git Submodule:**
:   If you really need to track upstream, or if you want to track and use various plugins, you could setup the [.cmk/ repository]({{jinja.vars.cmk_plugins_repo_url}}) (or your fork of it) as a git submodule inside your project folder.

To check details about the version you're using:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk mk.stat | jq '{make_version, version, "compose.mk", bin}'
```
```json {.cli_output}
{{bash('./compose.mk mk.stat 2>/dev/null | jq \'{make_version, version, "compose.mk", bin}\'')}}
```

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk docker.stat
```
```json {.cli_output}
{
  "docker_bin": "/usr/bin/docker",
  "docker_version": "28.2.2",
  "compose_version": "2.36.2",
  "container_count": "9",
  "socket": "unix:///var/run/docker.sock",
  "context_name": "default",
  "env": {...}
}
```

